# Stop a mouse cursor appearing on the panel.
#
# THE PROBLEM
# The PocketTerm35's RP2040 presents a COMPOSITE USB HID device: a keyboard
# collection (which is the only one Tracer uses — see tracerd/keymap.default.json,
# captured from hardware) and a mouse collection that reports nothing, because
# there is no pointing device on the unit. Touch arrives separately from the
# GT911 over I2C.
#
# That idle mouse collection is enough for libinput to enumerate a pointer, and
# a compositor with a pointer draws a cursor. On the panel that is a defect: the
# operator has no mouse, so an arrow sitting on screen is confusing and it
# obscures the UI.
#
# It is also not the only way a pointer can appear — see the vendor/model note
# below. Anything libinput calls a pointer produces the same defect, and cage
# parks it in the CENTRE of the output layout (seat.c seat_center_cursor()),
# where it has nothing to move it and so simply sits there.
#
# WHY CSS DOES NOT FIX IT
# tracer-ui already sets `cursor: none` unconditionally (tracer-ui/src/styles/
# tokens.css) and every element inherits it. That is a CLIENT instruction:
# Chromium can only act on it for its own surface, and only once the pointer has
# entered that surface. Nothing on this unit ever moves the pointer, so the
# compositor's own cursor can be drawn and never handed over to the client.
# The same CSS does hide the cursor under Raspberry Pi OS, where a real mouse is
# being moved — which is exactly why the CSS looked correct and was not enough.
#
# Removing the pointer at the source is the fix: no pointer device, no cursor,
# nothing for the compositor to draw.
#
# SAFETY
# Matched on CAPABILITY, never on a device node. This unit also carries the
# keyboard, and every button on it arrives through that device — ignoring the
# wrong interface would leave an operator with a panel they cannot navigate.
#
#   ID_INPUT_MOUSE=="1"      only an interface libinput sees as a pointer
#   ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD!="1"   never the keyboard, even if one node claims both
#   ID_INPUT_TOUCHSCREEN!="1" never the GT911; touch must keep working
#
# WHY THERE IS NO VENDOR/MODEL MATCH (removed 2026-09-04)
# This rule used to also require ID_VENDOR_ID=="1209" and ID_MODEL_ID=="0001",
# pinning it to the RP2040. On the flashed image a cursor still sat in the
# middle of the panel, which is proof the match never fired: cage only draws a
# cursor when the seat HAS pointer capability (seat.c update_capabilities()
# calls wlr_cursor_unset_image() otherwise), so a visible cursor means a
# pointer was still enumerated.
#
# Those two properties are the fragile part. Unlike ID_INPUT_*, they are not
# derived from the device itself — they come from
#   /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-persistent-input.rules:
#     SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", ENV{ID_BUS}=="", IMPORT{builtin}="usb_id"
# a GATED import that silently yields nothing if the guard does not hold, and
# they were only ever observed under Raspberry Pi OS, never on this image.
# ID_INPUT_MOUSE by contrast cannot be missing here: libinput's udev backend
# only adds devices that 60-input-id.rules has already tagged, so the keyboard
# and touchscreen working at all proves that rule ran.
#
# Dropping the pin is also the correct POLICY, not just the safer match. This
# is a sealed appliance with no pointing device: no pointer is ever legitimate,
# whoever makes it. The narrow match could not cover a USB mouse or a wireless
# dongle plugged in during bring-up — both of which enumerate a pointer that
# never moves, and so park a cursor dead centre exactly like the RP2040's idle
# mouse collection does. The guards above, not the vendor ID, are what keep
# this safe, and they are unchanged.
#
# HOW THE CURSOR IS DRAWN AT ALL
# Do not try to fix this by starving the compositor of a cursor theme. wlroots
# 0.18's wlr_xcursor_theme_load() falls back to load_default_theme(), a bitmap
# compiled into the library. This image already has no usable theme on disk —
# /usr/share/icons/default inherits DMZ-White, which is not installed — and a
# cursor appeared regardless. Removing the pointer is the only fix that holds.
#
# RE-VERIFYING ON HARDWARE
#   for d in /dev/input/event*; do udevadm info --query=property "$d" \
#     | grep -qE '^ID_INPUT_MOUSE=1' && echo "$d is a pointer"; done
# On a correct image that prints nothing at all. To see what is being ignored
# rather than just that nothing survives, drop the grep and read the whole
# property list — LIBINPUT_IGNORE_DEVICE=1 should be on any pointer node.

SUBSYSTEM=="input", ENV{ID_INPUT_MOUSE}=="1", \
  ENV{ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD}!="1", ENV{ID_INPUT_TOUCHSCREEN}!="1", \
  ENV{LIBINPUT_IGNORE_DEVICE}="1"
